  "rationale": "The requirement states one structural obligation. `impl Sources` must have `pub async fn search_structure(&self, query: &StructuralQuery) -> Result<StructuralMatches, SourceError>`.\n\n- **Signature:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[search_structure]` is a `pub async fn` in `impl Sources`. It takes `&self` and `query: &StructuralQuery`, and returns `Result<StructuralMatches, SourceError>`. This matches the requirement exactly.\n- **Types:**\n  - `StructuralMatches` is `pub type StructuralMatches = SearchResults<StructuralMatch>` in the same file.\n  - `StructuralQuery` is a struct in that file.\n  - `SourceError` is defined there too, with `Display`, `Error` and `From<io::Error>` impls.\n- **Delegation:** The function calls `self.analysis.search_structure(query).await`. The `analysis` field is `SourceAnalysis<LocalSources>`.\n- **Error type:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#impl[SourceAnalysis<P>]/fn[search_structure]` returns `Result<StructuralMatches, P::Error>`. In `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#impl[LocalSources/SourceProvider]/type[Error]`, `LocalSources` sets `type Error = SourceError`. The delegated result therefore has exactly the required type, and the delegation compiles with that signature.\n- **References:** Both items carry the `{impl}` reference to the requirement. The `SourceAnalysis` method is the delegate. It is arguably redundant as a root, but it does no harm.\n\nI found no structural gap and no acknowledgments apply. The requirement states only a signature, so there is no behavioral obligation to assess.",
